{"context":"delivery lifecycle standards are standalone executables that the Anvil Runtime (Core) invokes to run lifecycle operations — migrations, cache operations, rollback — on production servers (ADR-021 §3.4). Adopting a standard means executing its code with production privileges. v1.x distribution is a closed set of binaries built from the Core repository; v2 distribution is open and registry-driven, which enlarges the trust surface. Source: ADR-022-standard-trust-and-supply-chain-security.md Date:2026-08-03 Status:Proposed.","decision":"Adopt the standard trust and supply-chain security model specified in ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §8 (ADR-022 row) and §11.1:\n\n- **Integrity verification at install.** Every standard is verified for integrity when installed; installation fails if verification does not pass.\n- **Publisher attestation.** Standards carry attestation from their publisher, sufficient to establish origin; publishing with sufficient attestation is a standard responsibility (§5.6).\n- **Version pinning.** Adoptions pin standard versions; resolution is explicit and recorded.\n- **No auto-update for server-side standards.** Server-side standards are never updated automatically; an update is an explicit adoption event.\n- **Trust from the start.** Trust fields are part of the registry metadata schema from the outset — intr","consequences":"**Positive:** the Critical supply-chain risk is mitigated at install and update time; adoptions are reproducible and auditable (pinned versions + attestation); trust and compatibility are validated together at adoption (ADR-023).\n\n**Negative:** every install — including offline/bundled installs — requires verification material (checksums, attestation); the registry metadata schema must carry trust fields from day one (ADR-023); publishers must attest releases, adding process overhead for standard maintainers.","alternativesConsidered":"Considered\n\n- **No trust model (status quo).** Rejected: with open registry distribution, the Critical supply-chain risk (§11.1) is unacceptable.\n- **Trust retrofitted after the registry launches.** Rejected: the plan explicitly introduces trust with the registry, not retrofitted (§6.5).\n- **Auto-update with signature checking.** Rejected: the plan specifies no auto-update for server-side standards; silent updates would violate deterministic execution and explicit transitions.\n- **Trust only for"}